# If you can't fix every distortion, should you fix one?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What does the theory of the second best say? Why can removing one distortion make things worse?"
next: {"title":"How much would a planner need to know to set the right tax?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/54-how-much-would-a-planner-need-to-know-to.cast"}

## A free bus?
sources:
    - id: cats2017
      title: 'The prospects of fare-free public transport: evidence from Tallinn'
      authors: Oded Cats, Yusak O. Susilo, Triin Reimal
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Tallinn made public transport free for registered residents from January 2013. A travel-diary survey of 1,500 households before and about a year after found public transport use up 14%; walking trips fell far more than car trips. Transportation 44: 1083-1104.'

    path road #8f887c points [[60,400],[940,400]] hidden true
    path lane dashed #8f887c points [[60,225],[940,225]] hidden true
    icon town size 110 x 935 y 460 or ["building","city"] icon_look picture of "office building" hidden true
    icon car1 size 110 x 400 y 445 or ["automobile"] icon_look picture of car hidden true
    icon car2 size 110 x 500 y 445 or ["automobile"] icon_look picture of car hidden true
    icon car3 size 110 x 600 y 445 or ["automobile"] icon_look picture of car hidden true
    icon car4 size 110 x 700 y 445 or ["automobile"] icon_look picture of car hidden true
    icon car5 size 110 x 800 y 445 or ["automobile"] icon_look picture of car hidden true
    icon bus size 140 x 210 y 265 or ["bus front"] icon_look picture of bus hidden true
    text roadlbl "road" #8f887c x 890 y 375 font_size 26 hidden true
    text buslbl "bus lane" #8f887c x 120 y 195 font_size 26 hidden true
    icon tram size 110 x 170 y 575 or ["train"] icon_look picture of tram hidden true
    text tallinn "Tallinn, 2013:\nfree buses and trams\nfor residents" cites ["cats2017"] x 470 y 575 font_size 36 hidden true
    text zero "fare: €0" #2f6b8f cites ["cats2017"] x 820 y 575 font_size 44 hidden true
    text rule "price = cost?" #2f6b8f x 470 y 595 font_size 40 hidden true
    text waste "a subsidy wastes surplus" #3d3833 x 470 y 540 font_size 36 hidden true
    text delay "each car: $2 of delay to others" #b5482e x 600 y 540 font_size 34 hidden true
    path gate #b5482e points [[285,400],[285,520],[285,500],[130,500]] hidden true
    text tollt "toll $2" #b5482e x 210 y 528 font_size 32 hidden true
    text notoll "no toll allowed" #b5482e x 205 y 365 font_size 30 hidden true
    text cost "cost: $3 a ride" #2f6b8f x 460 y 275 font_size 38 hidden true
    mark x_gate around gate kind cross hidden true

    card title "A free bus?"

If you can't fix everything that's wrong with a market, should you at least fix what you can?
    draw road lane car1 car2 car3 car4 car5 bus town

B: Sounds like an easy y (@draw tram@)es. Here's a case that makes it harder. In January 2013, Tallinn, (@draw tallinn@) the capital of Estonia, made its buses and trams free for the people who live there.

Free. The whole cost of every ride paid out of taxes.
    draw zero

    erase tram tallinn zero

B: And the textbook frowns. A ride costs someth (@draw rule@)ing to run, so the fare should cover  (@highlight bus@)that cost, or people take rides worth less to them than they cost.

That's the usual case against subsidies (@draw waste@): like a tax, a subsidy pushes a market away from its efficient quantity and wastes s (@highlight waste effect underline@)urplus.

    erase rule waste

But a bus has a neighbour: the road. Take Harbourtown's one road into th (@highlight car1 car2 car3 car4 car5 effect box@)e centre, jammed every morning.
    draw roadlbl buslbl parallel true

B: Every car that joins the queue slows all the others down. Say, illustratively, each car tri (@draw delay@)p costs the other drivers two dollars of lost time, and nobody pays for it.
    move car1 car2 car3 car4 car5 by [-40,0] duration 2

The clean fix is a two-dollar toll. But say the road belongs to (@draw x_gate@) the state, and the town isn't allowed to (@draw notoll@) toll it. That distortion is stuck.
    draw gate tollt

B: What the harbour council does run is the bus. E (@draw cost@)ach ride costs it three dollars.

So what should the fare be? The textbook says three dollars, the full cost. Make your own guess.
    highlight bus cost

    quiz "What fare should the council charge for the bus?" judge false pool false
        right: Keep your guess in mind: the next figure puts every fare to the test.
        * $3, the full cost
        * $2
        * $1
        * Free

## Find the top of the hill
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 3.2
    'y':
      - -320
      - 170
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 215
      w: 510
      h: 430
vars:
    sub:
      value: 0
      min: 0
      max: 3
    sh:
      value: 0.5
      fixed: true
    fare:
      expr: 3-sub
    road:
      expr: 400*sh*sub
    waste:
      expr: 100*sub^2
    net:
      expr: 400*sh*sub-100*sub^2

    axes ax y_label "town's gain ($ per morning)" cross corner ticks.x [0,1,2,3] ticks.y [-300,-200,-100,0,100] hidden true
    text xl "bus subsidy per ride ($)" #3d3833 x 365 y 192 font_size 28 hidden true
    curve zero dashed #8f887c expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 3.2 hidden true
    curve hill thick #2f6b8f expr "400*sh*x - 100*x^2" x_from 0 x_to 3 hidden true
    dot p0 #8f887c at.x 0 at.y 0 hidden true
    dot dot #b5482e at.on hill at.x 0 bind {"at.x":"sub"} hidden true
    text l0 "fare $3" #8f887c at.data [0.45,-45] font_size 28 hidden true
    scratch calc work ["$1 off: +200 riders","half drove: 100 cars","100 × $2 = +200","waste: ½ × $1 × 200","= −100","net: +100"] x 805 y 440 font_size 28 hidden true
    text readout "fare: ${fare:2}\nroad: +{road:0}\nbus: −{waste:0}\ngain: {net:0}" #3d3833 x 840 y 470 font_size 34 hidden true
    text free "free bus: −300" #b5482e at.data [1.85,-235] font_size 32 hidden true
    text best "best fare: $2" #2f6b8f at.data [2.05,120] font_size 36 hidden true
    scratch mathcard center work ["x: subsidy",{"tex":"cdk\\,x - \\tfrac{1}{2}k\\,x^2"},{"tex":"x^* = cd = 1"}] font_size 36 hidden true

    card title "Find the top of the hill"

B: Three dollars is the textbook fare. To test it, plot the town's total gain against the bus subsidy, how far the fare drops below three dollars.
    draw ax xl zero

At zero subsidy the fare  (@draw p0 l0@)is three dollars, and we count the gain from there: zero.

B: Now take a dollar off the fare. (@draw calc_box calc_line_1@) Say, illustratively, that brings two hundred more riders each morning.

And here's the link between the markets: say  (@draw calc_line_2@)half of them used to drive. A hundred fewer cars, at two dollars eac (@draw calc_line_3@)h, saves the road two hundred dollars.

B: The cost: each new rider values the ride at less than the three dollars it costs to run. The w (@draw calc_line_4@)aste is a triangle: half of one dollar times two hundred, a (@draw calc_line_5@) hundred dollars.

So two hundred gained, a hundred (@draw calc_line_6@) lost. The 'distorted' far (@draw dot@)e lea (@animate sub 1 duration 1.5@)ves the town a hundred dollars better off.

    fade p0 l0 xl to 0.35

    erase calc

B: Then why stop at one dollar? Try it: slide the subsidy and find the top of the hill.
    draw hill readout

    explore params ["sub"]

It tops out at one doll (@highlight dot effect circle@)ar. Past that, each extra dollar of subsidy wastes more on the bus than it saves on the road.
    animate sub 1 duration 1.2

B: And the free bus, a three-dollar subsidy? Minus three hund (@draw free@)red. Worse than charging the full cost.
    animate sub 3 duration 2.5

So the best fare is two dollars: n (@draw best@)either the textbook's full cost nor free. The road decides it, a market the bus never touches directly.
    animate sub 1 duration 1.5

    erase free

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq store m default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq readout

    fade ax zero hill p0 l0 dot best xl to 0.15

Call the subsidy x. Say each dollar off the fare brings k more riders, a share d of them out of cars, and each car does c of harm on the road.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

B: Then the road gains c times d times k for every dollar of subsidy, and the bus wastes a triangle, a half times k times the subsidy squared.
    draw mathcard_line_2

The top is where the two slopes match: the best subsidy is c times d, two dollars times a half. And k cancels out.
    draw mathcard_line_3

    erase mathcard

    draw readout

@after

    erase mathq

    fade ax zero hill p0 l0 dot best xl to 1

    fade p0 l0 to 0.35

## How linked are the markets?
domain:
    x:
      - 0
      - 3.2
    'y':
      - -350
      - 450
    box:
      x: 110
      'y': 215
      w: 510
      h: 430
vars:
    sh:
      value: 0.5
      min: 0
      max: 1
    pct:
      expr: 100*sh
    best:
      expr: 2*sh
    top:
      expr: 400*sh^2
sources:
    - id: cats2017
      title: 'The prospects of fare-free public transport: evidence from Tallinn'
      authors: Oded Cats, Yusak O. Susilo, Triin Reimal
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Travel-diary survey of 1,500 Tallinn households before and about a year after fare-free transit (2013): public transport use up 14%; the shift came largely from walking, with only a small fall in car trips. Transportation 44: 1083-1104.'
    - id: lipsey1956
      title: The General Theory of Second Best
      authors: R. G. Lipsey, Kelvin Lancaster
      year: 1956
      finding: 'If one of the conditions for a Pareto optimum cannot be fulfilled, the other conditions are, in general, no longer desirable; the second-best optimum generally requires departures from all of them. Review of Economic Studies 24(1): 11-32.'
    - id: viner1950
      title: The Customs Union Issue
      authors: Jacob Viner
      year: 1950
      finding: A customs union can create trade (cheaper partner supply replaces dearer home production) or divert it (a dearer partner replaces the cheapest outside supplier), so a partial move to free trade can lower welfare.

    axes ax y_label "town's gain ($ per morning)" cross corner ticks.x [0,1,2,3] ticks.y [-200,0,200,400] hidden true
    text xl "bus subsidy per ride ($)" #3d3833 x 365 y 192 font_size 28 hidden true
    curve zero dashed #8f887c expr "0" x_from 0 x_to 3.2 hidden true
    curve hill thick #2f6b8f expr "400*sh*x - 100*x^2" x_from 0 x_to 3 hidden true
    dot dot #b5482e at.on hill at.x 1 bind {"at.x":"best"} hidden true
    text readout "from cars: {pct:0}%\nbest subsidy: ${best:2}\nbest gain: {top:0}" #3d3833 x 815 y 470 font_size 32 hidden true
    scale guess x 685 y 250 width 260 font_size 28 label "best subsidy ($)?" min 0 max 3 value 2 ticks 3 hidden true
    text tallinn "Tallinn: walking fell\nfar more than driving" #8a5fa8 cites ["cats2017"] x 815 y 270 font_size 30 hidden true
    text lfree "free bus:\nfar below ↓" #b5482e at.data [2.6,-250] font_size 28 hidden true
    text thm "One condition broken?\nThe others stop\nbeing the target." #3d3833 cites ["lipsey1956"] x 800 y 360 font_size 32 hidden true
    text thmsrc "Lipsey & Lancaster, 1956" #8f887c cites ["lipsey1956"] x 800 y 265 font_size 28 hidden true
    text viner "Viner, 1950:\ncustoms unions" #8a5fa8 cites ["viner1950"] x 800 y 190 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "How linked are the markets?"

The best subsidy came out at one dollar, and it rested on one assumption: that  (@draw readout@)half the new riders used to drive.
    draw ax xl zero hill dot

B: So what if all of them did?
    highlight readout part "from cars"

Every rider taken  (@draw guess@)off the road now saves two dollars. Guess where the best subsidy moves.

    ask "If every new rider used to drive, where is the best subsidy? Click on the line." on guess store g default "1"
        right: You said ${g}. Watch the top of the hill.
        wrong: You said ${g}. Watch the top of the hill.

    erase guess

B: To two dollars. The hill grows, and its top slides right: the stronger the link to the road, the bigger the subsidy should be.
    animate sh 1 duration 4.5 ghost true

    erase hill_ghost dot_ghost

And if none of them used to drive, if they all used to walk? Then the hill is gone. The best subsidy is zero, and the textbook fare is right again.
    animate sh 0 duration 5

    erase readout

B: That's not just a thought experiment. In Tallinn, a survey  (@draw tallinn@)of households found that trips on foot fell far more than trips by car.
    animate sh 0.15 duration 1.5

So a free fare there looks far past the top of the h (@highlight dot effect circle@)ill, at least as a fix for traffic.
    draw lfree

    erase tallinn lfree

    draw readout

B: Try it: slide the share that comes from cars, and watch the best fare move.
    animate sh 0.5 duration 1.5

    explore params ["sh"]

    animate sh 0.5 duration 1

    erase readout

In 1956 Richard Lipsey and Kelvin Lancaster made this a genera (@draw thm thmsrc@)l theorem: when one condition for efficiency can't be met, the others are, in general, no longer the right target.

B: The idea grew out of trade. In 1950 J (@draw viner@)acob Viner showed that dropping tariffs on one neighbour alone can make a country poorer, if (@highlight viner@) it switches from the cheapest seller to the neighbour.

So a step toward the textbook ideal isn't always a step uphill. Which way is up depends on how the markets are linked.
    highlight hill thm

## A licence for anything?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Lipsey and Lancaster would sign that.
    good: Second-best thinking is slippery; you held on.
    poor: Look for the link to the stuck distortion first.
    none: The theorem waits. So does the jammed road.
sources:
    - id: bhagwati1963
      title: Domestic Distortions, Tariffs and the Theory of Optimum Subsidy
      authors: Jagdish Bhagwati, V. K. Ramaswami
      year: 1963
      finding: 'A domestic distortion is best corrected by a tax or subsidy aimed at its source, not by a tariff; the targeting principle. Journal of Political Economy 71(1): 44-50.'
    - id: parry2009
      title: Should Urban Transit Subsidies Be Reduced?
      authors: Ian W. H. Parry, Kenneth A. Small
      year: 2009
      finding: 'With congestion, pollution and accident externalities and scale economies, the large transit fare subsidies of Washington DC, Los Angeles and London are generally efficient; small fare cuts raise welfare in most cases even from fares at about half of operating cost. American Economic Review 99(3): 700-724.'

