    '|b||Then why stop at one dollar? Try it: slide the subsidy and find the top of the hill.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5480
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:14vx5s0'
    '|a||It tops out at one dollar. Past that, each extra dollar of subsidy wastes more on the bus than it saves on the road.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8200
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||And the free bus, a three-dollar subsidy? Minus three hundred. Worse than charging the full cost.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6160
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:vx0do6'
    '|a||So the best fare is two dollars: neither the textbook''s full cost nor free. The road decides it, a market the bus never touches directly.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10000
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:mreykv'
    '|a||Show the math, or skip it?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 1520
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:uoxifv'
    '|a||Call the subsidy x. Say each dollar off the fare brings k more riders, a share d of them out of cars, and each car does c of harm on the road.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11760
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||Then the road gains c times d times k for every dollar of subsidy, and the bus wastes a triangle, a half times k times the subsidy squared.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11040
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:19jp8h4'
    '|a||The top is where the two slopes match: the best subsidy is c times d, two dollars times a half. And k cancels out.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8840
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:16jzrua'
    '|a||The best subsidy came out at one dollar, and it rested on one assumption: that half the new riders used to drive.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7760
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||So what if all of them did?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:fo6o4y'
    '|a||Every rider taken off the road now saves two dollars. Guess where the best subsidy moves.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5360
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:ibsu0b'
    '|a||If every new rider used to drive, where is the best subsidy? Click on the line.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6080
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:dhekp'
    '|b||To two dollars. The hill grows, and its top slides right: the stronger the link to the road, the bigger the subsidy should be.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6520
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||And if none of them used to drive, if they all used to walk? Then the hill is gone. The best subsidy is zero, and the textbook fare is right again.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9720
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:1s8trc9'
    '|b||That''s not just a thought experiment. In Tallinn, a survey of households found that trips on foot fell far more than trips by car.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8200
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1tx03nt'
    '|a||So a free fare there looks far past the top of the hill, at least as a fix for traffic.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6040
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:5h000b'
    '|b||Try it: slide the share that comes from cars, and watch the best fare move.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4680
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:1ofvf1j'
    '|a||In 1956 Richard Lipsey and Kelvin Lancaster made this a general theorem: when one condition for efficiency can''t be met, the others are, in general, no longer the right target.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11520
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||The idea grew out of trade. In 1950 Jacob Viner showed that dropping tariffs on one neighbour alone can make a country poorer, if it switches from the cheapest seller to the neighbour.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11320
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:cgnpcw'
    '|a||So a step toward the textbook ideal isn''t always a step uphill. Which way is up depends on how the markets are linked.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7480
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:aws39b'
    '|b||Here''s what bothers me. If any market can be linked to some other distortion, then every lobby has an argument.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7520
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Farmers, car makers, airlines: subsidise us, and we''ll offset a problem somewhere else.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5880
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:9gb8ni'
    '|b||Exactly. The theorem says the textbook rule may fail. It doesn''t say which way to go.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5680
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:qbcxla'
    '|a||True, and that''s why economists add a second rule: fix a distortion at its source when you can. In 1963 Jagdish Bhagwati and V. K. Ramaswami made it precise for trade.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12440
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:z0qa9e'
    '|b||Here the source is the road. A toll fixes the jam itself; the bus subsidy only claws back part of it, and only through the riders who would have driven.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8520
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:11enw31'
    '|a||So how sure are we? The theorem is mathematics: consensus.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5120
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:12gw1zq'
    '|b||That big transit subsidies can pay is mostly agreed: Ian Parry and Kenneth Small found the large fare subsidies in Washington, Los Angeles and London generally efficient.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10760
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:z52zkq'
    '|a||But how strong the links between markets really are, city by city and tax by tax, is contested.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6560
      voice: 'gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy #ctx:xm7asa'
    '|b||Three new cases now, to try it yourself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3000
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:fkx8n0'
    '|b||A power plant''s smoke cannot be taxed. Electric heaters run on its power. What should happen to heaters?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6120
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow #ctx:u7srr'
    '|b||A small tax. Heaters add to the plant''s output, so they are linked to the untaxed smoke, the other way round from the bus.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8440
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||Say each car downtown does $4 of jam that can''t be tolled. A park-and-ride lot at the edge pulls in new users, but only 1 in 4 of them would have driven downtown. Roughly what subsidy per user?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 15000
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|a||$1: the subsidy is worth the harm only for the share it pulls off the road, $4 times a quarter.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6800
      voice: gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy
    '|b||A monopolist already makes too little, and its smoke is untaxed. Is a tax equal to the full harm of the smoke the best tax?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8840
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|b||Not necessarily. The tax cuts output that monopoly power already holds too low, so the best tax can be smaller than the harm.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8360
      voice: 'gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow'
    '|a||So, if you can''t fix every distortion, should you fix one? Only once you know how it''s linked to the ones you can''t fix, and how much a government can know about that is the next question.':
